rset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2-3x}=2$  is $x=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186067576 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067578 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186067576 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547373 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186067576 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067579 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547985 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547986 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548059 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547988 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548017 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068735 1039 "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186068735 1039 "Finding the Indefinite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068801 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547974 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068801 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548004 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068801 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548055 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186068957 1050 1500.0 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186069401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186069401 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\ln^{3} z}{z}dz$ using integration by parts." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186069401 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z$ using integration by parts." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186069401 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡(e)-{ln}^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186069630 1061 1752 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186069630 1061 640946 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186069631 1061 1753 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186069631 1061 640947 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186069632 1061 1757 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186069632 1061 640950 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186069725 1061 27070 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186069725 1061 640953 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186070032 1064 "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}x & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}x(\\operatorname{ln}x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{2}x-I \\\\ I+I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ 2I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186070032 1064 "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x-I \\\\ I+I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 2I & ={ln}^{2}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186075756 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547471 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186075756 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547471 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186078464 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548025 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186078464 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548048 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186078465 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548031 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186078466 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547995 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186078808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186078808 1071 "Consider the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n-17,\\,-12,\\,-7,\\,-2,\\,…\n$$\n\nWhat is the $24$th term of this sequence?\n\n${a}_{24}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186078808 1071 "Consider the following arithmetic sequence.\n$-17,-12,-7,-2, …$\nWhat is the $24$th term of this sequence?$a_{24} =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186078808 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n-17,\\,-12,\\,-7,\\,-2,\\,…,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=-17$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=-12-(-17)=5.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-17+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =-17+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $24$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=24:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =5n-22 \\\\ {a}_{24} & =5(24)-22 \\\\  & =120-22 \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186083325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548044 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186087787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548058 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186087794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548037 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186092182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547972 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186092182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548019 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186092254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548022 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186092256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548001 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186092256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548047 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186092269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548024 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186092373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548021 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186094735 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094736 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186094735 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094737 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186094735 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094738 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186094735 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094739 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186094735 1075 #edb/ref 17592186094740 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186094735 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547340 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186094735 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547354 13194139535953