6479 1074 "For this integral we choose $u = x^4-3x$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\cos(x).$ Filling in the table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^4-3x$ | $\\cos(x)$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 4x^3-3$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} \\sin(x)$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} (x^4-3x)\\sin(x)$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 12x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}   -\\cos(x)$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}  -(4x^3-3)(-\\cos(x))$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} 24x$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1}  -\\sin(x)$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 12x^2(-\\sin(x))$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0.5,1} 24$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} \\cos(x)$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}- 24x\\cos(x)$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0.5,1}  \\sin(x)$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0.5,1}+$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0.5,1}  24\\sin(x)$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{4}-3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =({x}^{4}-3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+(4{x}^{3}-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & \\,\\,-12{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-24x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C \\\\  & =({x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}-3x+24)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & \\,\\,+(4{x}^{3}-24x-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096482 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int (x^2-1)(x+2)^5 \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186096482 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int (x^2-1)(x+2)^5 \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096482 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u = x^2-1$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} =(x+2)^5\n$$\n\nFilling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^2-1$ | $(x+2)^5$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 2x$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}\\dfrac{(x+2)^6}{6}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}\\dfrac{(x^2-1)(x+2)^6}{6}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 2$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} \\dfrac{(x+2)^7}{42}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -\\,\\dfrac{x(x+2)^7}{21}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1}\\dfrac{(x+2)^8}{336}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} \\dfrac{(x+2)^8}{168}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(x^{2} - 1)(x + 2)^{5}dx &= ((x^{2} - 1)(x + 2)^{6})/(6) - (x(x + 2)^{7})/(21) + ((x + 2)^{8})/(168) + C \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)^{6})/(168)(28(x^{2} - 1) - 8x(x + 2) + (x + 2)^{2}) + C \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)^{6})/(168)(21x^{2} - 12x - 24) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186096482 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u = x^2-1$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} =(x+2)^5.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^2-1$ | $(x+2)^5$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 2x$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}\\dfrac{(x+2)^6}{6}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}\\dfrac{(x^2-1)(x+2)^6}{6}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 2$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} \\dfrac{(x+2)^7}{42}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -\\,\\dfrac{x(x+2)^7}{21}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1}\\dfrac{(x+2)^8}{336}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1}  \\dfrac{(x+2)^8}{168}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}-1)(x+2)^{5}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)(x+2)^{6}}{6}-\\frac{x(x+2)^{7}}{21}+\\frac{(x+2)^{8}}{168}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{6}}{168}(28({x}^{2}-1)-8x(x+2)+(x+2)^{2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{6}}{168}(21{x}^{2}-12x-24)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547979 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548028 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548032 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548035 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548067 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547973 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547976 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548049 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548060 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548002 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548069 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548073 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548039 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548075 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548076 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548036 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548080 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186096911 1050 840.0 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186097127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186097127 1071 "Evaluate  $\\int (4{x}^{4}+2){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186097127 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int(4x^{4} + 2)e^{x}dx$." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186097127 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u=4{x}^{4}+2$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4{x}^{4}+2$ | ${e}^{x}$ |  |  |\n| $16{x}^{3}$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $+$ | $(4{x}^{4}+2){e}^{x}$ |\n| $48{x}^{2}$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $-$ | $-16{x}^{3}{e}^{x}$ |\n| $96x$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $+$ | $48{x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ |\n| $96$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $-$ | $-96x{e}^{x}$ |\n| $0$ | ${e}^{x}$ | $+$ | $96{e}^{x}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{4}+2){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{x}(4{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}+48{x}^{2}-96x+98)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186097928 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int x^3e^{x/2} \\textrm{d}x$. We can do it by applying integration by parts three times, but the calculation is pretty long. How can we speed it up?\n\nMultiple applications of integration by parts becomes quick and easy if we use the **tabular method**.\n\nLet's start by picking our $u$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas normal:\n\n$$\nu = x^3\\,,\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x/2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe put this in a table, as shown below:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n\nTo fill in the second row of the table, we differentiate the function in the first column and integrate the function in the second column. Doing this, we find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^3\\right) = 3x^2\\,,\\quad \\int e^{x/2} \\textrm{d}x = 2e^{x/2}\\,\n$$\n\nFilling in our table, we get the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ |  |\n\nAt this step, we begin filling the third column. It always starts with a \"$+$\" and then the signs alternate at each subsequent step.\n\nWe continue by differentiating the expression in the first column and integrating the expression in the second column until we get zero in the first column.\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 6x$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 4e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} 6$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 8e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}16e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}$ |\n\nNow we multiply the same colored terms along with the same colored sign. Specifically we multiply $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ with ${\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} 2e^{x/2}}$, $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}3x^2$ with $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}4e^{x/2}$, and so on. We get\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} 2x^3e^{x/2}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 6x$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 4e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -12x^2e^{x/2}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} 6$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 8e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 48xe^{x/2}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}16e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -96e^{x/2}$ |\n\nFinally, we add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intx^{3}e^{x/2}dx &= 2x^{3}e^{x/2} - 12x^{2}e^{x/2} + 48xe^{x/2} - 96e^{x/2} + C \\\\\n&= e^{x/2}(2x^{3} - 12x^{2} + 48x - 96) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186097928 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int x^3e^{x/2} \\textrm{d}x.$ We can do it by applying integration by parts three times, but the calculation is pretty long. How can we speed it up?\n\nMultiple applications of integration by parts become quick and easy if we use the **tabular method**.\n\nLet's start by picking our $u$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}$ as normal:\n\n$$\nu = x^3\\,,\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x/2}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe put this in a table, as shown below:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n\nTo fill in the second row of the table, we differentiate the function in the first column and integrate the function in the second column. Doing this, we find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^3\\right) = 3x^2\\,,\\quad \\int e^{x/2} \\textrm{d}x = 2e^{x/2}\\,.\n$$\n\nFilling in our table, we get the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ |  |\n\nAt this step, we begin filling the third column. It always starts with a \"$+$\" and then the signs alternate at each subsequent step.\n\nWe continue by differentiating the expression in the first column and integrating the expression in the second column until we get zero in the first column.\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}  x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 6x$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}  4e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} 6$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 8e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}16e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}$ |\n\nNow we multiply the same-colored terms along with the same-colored sign. Specifically we multiply $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ with ${\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} 2e^{x/2}},$ $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}3x^2$ with $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}4e^{x/2},$ and so on. We get\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}  x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} 2x^3e^{x/2}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 6x$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}  4e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}  -12x^2e^{x/2}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} 6$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 8e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 48xe^{x/2}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}16e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -96e^{x/2}$ |\n\nFinally, we add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x/2}\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{3}{e}^{x/2}-12{x}^{2}{e}^{x/2}+48x{e}^{x/2}-96{e}^{x/2}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{x/2}(2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+48x-96)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099362 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099363 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099364 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099365 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099366 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547375 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547394 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547407 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547503 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186099361 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547704 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186099361 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547407 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186100806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548006 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186100806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548051 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186100807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548014 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186100893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547977 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547982 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186100986 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547997 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186100987 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547994 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186101361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186101361 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(8, 12)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$. Which of the following points must lie on $y = 2f(x)$?" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186101361 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(8,12)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=2f(x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186101361 1074 "The function $y=2f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(8,12)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(8,2\\cdot 12)=(8,24),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=2f(x).$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186110613 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548056 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186110614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548065 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186110615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548010 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186115200 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548013 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186115219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547999 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186115264 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547989 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186115264 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548062 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186115266 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548020 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186115417 1050 420.0 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119528 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. What is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,-\\,4} f(x)$?\n\n![](images/ab/ab2abe13314c2005907dcd5d7077af8e87b7126474bbb0261f757d8ec7c00698.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186119528 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ What is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,-\\,4} f(x)?$\n\n![](images/ab/ab2abe13314c2005907dcd5d7077af8e87b7126474bbb0261f757d8ec7c00698.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119528 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^+} f(x)=\\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, the function $f(x)$ is not defined for $x<-4$, and consequently we cannot approach $x=-4$ from the left. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^-} f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4} f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186119528 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^+} f(x)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function $f(x)$ is not defined for $x<-4,$ and consequently we cannot approach $x=-4$ from the left. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4^-} f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -4} f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119535 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,4} f(x)$.\n\n![](images/16/168433761f6629ad555a70acf5764f93246484c253f3c7ecba8fdfdfd87c2adf.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186119535 1071 "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\,4} f(x).$\n\n![](images/16/168433761f6629ad555a70acf5764f93246484c253f3c7ecba8fdfdfd87c2adf.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119535 1074 "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $4$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty$. So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^-} f(x) = -\\infty \\,\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $4$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty$. So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^+} f(x) = -\\infty \\,\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4} f(x)=-\\infty \\,\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186119535 1074 "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $4$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^-} f(x) = -\\infty \\, .\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $4$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4^+} f(x) = -\\infty \\, .\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 4} f(x)=-\\infty \\, .\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548005 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548071 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547970 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186119871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548066 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186120022 1050 420.0 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186120993 1064 "Until now, all limits of the form $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ that we have encountered have evaluated to either a finite number or $\\text{DNE}$. However, there is another possibility: the limit could be *infinite*!\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. This function has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$, and the function value $f(x)$ grows without bound as $x$ approaches the asymptote $x=1$. What is the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x)$?\n\n![](images/ab/ab9d57d3952fe0221616f9e64e1295fd0511703bdd5ed3a5fe91113cc7c8bf1b.png)\n\nRemember that in order for computing any limit, in this case, $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x)$, we have to compute the left and right-sided limits separately and ensure that they match up.\n\n- **Left-sided limit:** As we approach $x=1$ (the vertical asymptote) from the left side of the graph, the function $f(x)$ increases without bound, approaching $\\infty$. We conclude that\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\infty$.\n- **Right-sided limit:** Likewise, as we approach the vertical asymptote $x=1$ from the right side of the graph, $f(x)$ still approaches $\\infty$. Consequently,\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^+} f(x) = \\infty$.\n\nWe see that both the left and right limits exist and are equal to $\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^+} f(x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nAs a result, the overall limit is also equal to $\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x) = \\infty\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186120993 1064 "Until now, all limits of the form $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ that we have encountered have evaluated to either a finite number or $\\text{DNE}.$ However, there is another possibility: the limit could be *infinite*!\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. This function has a vertical asymptote at $x=1,$ and the function value $f(x)$ grows without bound as $x$ approaches the asymptote $x=1.$ What is the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x)$? \n\n![](images/ab/ab9d57d3952fe0221616f9e64e1295fd0511703bdd5ed3a5fe91113cc7c8bf1b.png)\n\nRemember that in order for computing any limit, in this case, $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x),$ we have to compute the left and right-sided limits separately and ensure that they match up.\n\n- **Left-sided limit:** As we approach $x=1$ (the vertical asymptote) from the left side of the graph, the function $f(x)$ increases without bound, approaching $\\infty.$ We conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\infty.\n$$\n\n- **Right-sided limit:** Likewise, as we approach the vertical asymptote $x=1$ from the right side of the graph, $f(x)$ still approaches $\\infty.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^+ } f(x) = \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe see that both the left and right limits exist and are equal to $\\infty\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1^+} f(x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nAs a result, the overall limit is also equal to $\\infty\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 1} f(x) = \\infty\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186124654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548012 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186128914 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following is true when $x\\rightarrow 2$?\n\n1. $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$\n2. $f(x) \\rightarrow 2$\n3. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$\n\n![](images/33/33650d66a0f3a2fae762b1c19da952597740868b00eeec11a14ad822536962e8.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186128914 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following is true when $x\\rightarrow 2?$   \n\nI. $f(x) \\rightarrow  \\infty$\n\nII. $f(x) \\rightarrow  2$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\rightarrow  -\\infty$\n\n![](images/33/33650d66a0f3a2fae762b1c19da952597740868b00eeec11a14ad822536962e8.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186128914 1074 "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $2$, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\n![](images/45/4585f983dac68f3ac655fbaaed6481cd1479c4cffae2c10d9f08d991c6533f51.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186128914 1074 "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ \n\n![](images/45/4585f983dac68f3ac655fbaaed6481cd1479c4cffae2c10d9f08d991c6533f51.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186128919 1071 "![](images/d5/d5750d5909f8a6e7c893109dd2ebe1e6317e7e5505df52d8be8966b8f914f140.png)\n\nThe graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) is shown above. Which of the following is true when ![](images/cf/cf6469e6430ca528386d02c3c62c8856abdd9cd592dab391ca0b37cb9eee214d.svg)\n\nI. ![](images/61/61e37acf991ed5b1c42f3c21005513060e0168597a24ca4fc0f3925a75fe77ad.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/6a/6ac2f1940e7398cde2efc3364d99ccbcba9cb95c761f338c1c643daa557cf804.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/33/333a8ffca9eb9ad90f40e6a0a28a8119936d27bc840b0495c6b98eafec91d875.svg)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186128919 1071 "![](images/d5/d5750d5909f8a6e7c893109dd2ebe1e6317e7e5505df52d8be8966b8f914f140.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is true when $x \\rightarrow 1^{+}$?\n\n1. $f(x) \\rightarrow -2$\n2. $f(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$\n3. $f(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186128919 1074 "The notation ![](images/e1/e15c0b105ea984c36aeb1eae6f4e743fd66d4a152acf6f2fdc5a9f977760e39f.svg) means that ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) approaches ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) *from the right*.\n\n![](images/99/99421620750831182ef45a4cfda34ee4b36f772f88df5572783bffc3ea9580c5.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) approaches ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) from the right, the values of ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) increase, approaching ![](images/b7/b7dcbb40b0c8f515a6b1ee8773cbf301ab1f40446114fd0e5436b57fd08e3697.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/33/333a8ffca9eb9ad90f40e6a0a28a8119936d27bc840b0495c6b98eafec91d875.svg) as ![](images/57/57038ceb8b89a8ded3292cf53d2875f5da509c824f4ad1cacd8ce1616f1cfff5.svg) So, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186128919 1074 "The notation $x\\rightarrow 1^{+}$ means that $x$ approaches $1$ *from the right*.\n\n![](images/99/99421620750831182ef45a4cfda34ee4b36f772f88df5572783bffc3ea9580c5.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow 1^{+}$." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186128920 1074 "Recall that $x = a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y = f(x)$ if $f(x) \\to \\pm \\infty$ as $x \\to a^+$ or $x \\to a^-$.\n\n![](images/59/596435060b73c6df3b05bf5f56b4650c2e77ecb70203f3d680ad9d0dddc1f027.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$. Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty$.\n\nSo, we can write:\n\n- $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -1^-$\n- $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -1^+$\n\nThese give us the same asymptote $x = -1$." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186128920 1074 "Recall that $x = a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y = f(x)$ if $f(x) \\to \\pm \\infty$ as $x \\to a^+$ or $x \\to a^-.$\n\n![](images/59/596435060b73c6df3b05bf5f56b4650c2e77ecb70203f3d680ad9d0dddc1f027.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty.$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -1^-$\n\n- $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -1^+$\n\nThese give us the same asymptote $x = -1.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186128921 1071 "![](images/8a/8a28f89ca66be27ba85ded32668b7077cb2b152f9877fe52f6702a40be047773.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $b-a$, given the following:\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow a$\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow b$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186128921 1071 "![](images/8a/8a28f89ca66be27ba85ded32668b7077cb2b152f9877fe52f6702a40be047773.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $b-a,$ given the following:\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow a$\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow b$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186128921 1074 "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$, the values of $g(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty$.\n\n![](images/c5/c5b5723c08edacff5a29e160b225343f565d23bb764a84149ca20ebbe28d4ad3.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n$g(x) \\to -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -1$.\n\nTherefore, $a = -1$.\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$, the values of $g(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf9f0d83f05002952d531fe1dcaa6ad14d7d7c4216a59d9a2d3a4e621b57ef5.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n$g(x) \\to \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to 1$.\n\nTherefore, $b =1$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb - a = 1 - (-1) = 2\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186128921 1074 "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1,$ the values of $g(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty.$ \n\n![](images/c5/c5b5723c08edacff5a29e160b225343f565d23bb764a84149ca20ebbe28d4ad3.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\to -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a = -1.$\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the values of $g(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bf9f0d83f05002952d531fe1dcaa6ad14d7d7c4216a59d9a2d3a4e621b57ef5.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\to \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b =1.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb - a = 1 - (-1) = 2.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547971 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548052 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548029 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129103 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548043 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547980 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548045 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547978 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548061 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186129379 1050 420.0 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186130335 1064 "Sometimes, a function might increase or decrease without bound when its input gets close to a specific value.\n\nFor example, consider the function whose graph is shown below:\n\n![](images/21/2163d96abc422e376581a693a6270da9d45ccc79d426d89f7ba9d1b046e2a254.png)\n\nAs the input $x$ gets closer to $1$, the values of the function get larger and larger.\n\n![](images/c3/c367d69e8ef7369e81872f6f78460d8a2052a35e31d8f46e2b072d24a6d83f5a.png)\n\nWhen this happens, we say that the function approaches \"infinity\", or that it has an **infinite limit** at $x=1$. We can write this mathematically, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty\\quad \\text{as}\\quad x \\rightarrow 1\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $f(x)$ approaches infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\".\n\nNow, consider the function $y = g(x)$, shown below. This is an example of a function that *decreases* without bound.\n\n![](images/a2/a21c625314d96dca5e724064bc35e67bed7c61fd56acc993cd7d281da29a2062.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1$. So, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow 1\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186130335 1064 "Sometimes, a function might increase or decrease without bound when its input gets close to a specific value.\n\nFor example, consider the function whose graph is shown below:\n\n![](images/21/2163d96abc422e376581a693a6270da9d45ccc79d426d89f7ba9d1b046e2a254.png)\n\nAs the input $x$ gets closer to $1,$ the values of the function get larger and larger.\n\n![](images/c3/c367d69e8ef7369e81872f6f78460d8a2052a35e31d8f46e2b072d24a6d83f5a.png)\n\nWhen this happens, we say that the function approaches \"infinity\", or that it has an **infinite limit** at $x=1.$ We can write this mathematically, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty\\quad \\text{as}\\quad x \\rightarrow 1\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $f(x)$ approaches infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\".\n\nNow, consider the function $y = g(x),$ shown below. This is an example of a function that *decreases* without bound.\n\n![](images/a2/a21c625314d96dca5e724064bc35e67bed7c61fd56acc993cd7d281da29a2062.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1.$ So, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad  \\text{as } \\quad x \\rightarrow 1.\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186130336 1064 "We know that some functions approach infinity as $x$ approaches a certain value, like the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/da/da7878b503b9dff8f04c0f2c42e021353dc8b5fa744476fa0227bb954472c5ed.png)\n\nHere, the function shoots upwards when $x$ approaches $2$ *from the right*, that is, 