f(4)=f(1+3)=f(1)=F(1)=2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2(2)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+2)\\ne f(2)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186167274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186167274 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186167274 1071 "$⟨f(x), g(x)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}f(x)g(x)dx$\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x ∈ [0, 2π]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$⟨\\sin (4x), 1⟩ =$\n{{field-1}}\n\nThe functions $f(x) = \\sin (4x)$ and $g(x) = 1$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186167274 1074 "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186175921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548034 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186175948 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548008 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186175948 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548015 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186175948 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548040 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186175956 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548009 13194139535953 17592186045417 