{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186188793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186188793 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ and $E[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{5}{12},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186188793 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$f(x, y) = {x + y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nGiven that $E[X] = E[Y] = \\frac{7}{12}$, and $E[X^{2}] = \\frac{5}{12}$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186188793 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})\\cdot (\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{144} & -\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{144} & \\frac{11}{144}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186189409 1064 "For a set of random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$, the **covariance matrix** $\\Sigma$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X_{1}] & Cov[X_{1}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{1}, X_{n}] \\\\ Cov[X_{2}, X_{1}] & Var[X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{2}, X_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ Cov[X_{n}, X_{1}] & Cov[X_{n}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Var[X_{n}]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i]$.\n- The element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j]$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_j,X_i]\n$$\n\nfor any pair of random variables $X_i, X_j$, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The covariance matrix is always symmetric. Moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix.*" 13194139535953 17592186045417 false] [17592186189409 1064 "For a set of random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n,$ the **covariance matrix** $\\Sigma$ is defined as\n\n![](images/41/416ac73d7b76e9c62efead1278e7a2b47c00dd03a8af17df99f28aea99f3f7a0.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i].$\n\n- The element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j].$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_j,X_i]$ for any pair of random variables $X_i, X_j$, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The covariance matrix is always symmetric. Moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix.*" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186198354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547984 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186198354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548041 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186217109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547975 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186217109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548027 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186217110 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547990 13194139535953 17592186045417 t