7592186045417 true] [17592186547968 1089 #edb/ref 17592186547969 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547968 1090 #uuid "fb209e62-dbab-5627-b8de-306596211c79" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548083 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548088 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548097 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548134 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548137 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548138 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548166 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548188 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1085 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.$ Let $y=f(x)$ be the solution that satisfies the initial condition $y(0)=0.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1086 #uuid "606e3887-aa6c-5661-a09f-d03aff0342a6" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547969 1087 #edb/ref 17592186548138 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547918 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547970 1069 "q-102535" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547970 1071 "![](images/f4/f498fdcd8c6be58b8d1c1e6e4c3d835799a8c571cdd9125f7e7555307c94a6d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547970 1072 #uuid "017219c2-ceac-5f2c-bcc8-c754086bffcc" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547970 1074 "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nWe see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is true.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547854 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547971 1069 "q-66682" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547971 1071 "![](images/10/10ef921066e2984492588891cb36d52c142d387c94d23aa226e26377f30d96cd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to 0$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to 2$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to 2$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547971 1072 #uuid "0274893d-bdb8-5547-900e-3f5b62ab65d3" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547971 1074 "![](images/68/68343d3b77ebad519983a98cc35531a632c85e453cb38a4583fb21cf4ea620e0.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that:\n\n- as $x$ approaches $0$ the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty $\n\n- as $x$ approaches $2$ the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547885 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547972 1069 "q-78590" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547972 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}=$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547972 1072 #uuid "0515d331-a2ed-5378-ab7b-e5eca8ff6d75" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547972 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{27}{64})=\\frac{3}{16} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{81}{256})=\\frac{9}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{64})}{(\\frac{3}{16})}=\\frac{9}{64}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547945 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547973 1069 "q-14778" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547973 1071 "$\\int (4{x}^{4}+4){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547973 1072 #uuid "09b2a1cb-b590-5e4c-af65-76fb35a22b46" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547973 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u=4{x}^{4}+4$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4{x}^{4}+4$ | ${e}^{2x}$ |  |  |\n| $16{x}^{3}$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}$ | $+$ | $(2{x}^{4}+2){e}^{2x}$ |\n| $48{x}^{2}$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}$ | $-$ | $-4{x}^{3}{e}^{2x}$ |\n| $96x$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{8}$ | $+$ | $6{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}$ |\n| $96$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{16}$ | $-$ | $-6x{e}^{2x}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{32}$ | $+$ | $3{e}^{2x}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{4}+4){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{2x}(2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-6x+5)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547863 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547974 1069 "q-115003" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547974 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547974 1072 #uuid "0b786a85-3015-50d7-b6da-f38d91ed719a" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547974 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2(-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-2\\pi /2}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})))-(\\frac{{e}^{-2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547883 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547922 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547975 1069 "q-250075" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547975 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x-1$ and the line $y=-2x-13$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547975 1072 #uuid "0dbc8468-e68d-529c-9edc-f8593cda9fbb" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547975 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x-1 \\\\ y=-2x-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-1 & =-2x-13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-2{x}_{1}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-4)-13 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,-5).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =-2{x}_{2}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-3)-13 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-3,-7).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/78/782a5e0fbe4c362ca17e86a11f017f6909873cb1f06f0d801d6d958bd1c8ab44.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547919 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547976 1069 "q-13146" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547976 1071 "Evaluate $\\int (2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547976 1072 #uuid "1051456d-d936-5dd0-8e18-6d4a40dca9fc" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547976 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5$ | ${e}^{2x}$ |  |  |\n| $6{x}^{2}+6x$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x}}{2}$ |\n| $12x+6$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+3x){e}^{2x}}{2}$ |\n| $12$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{8}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{(6x+3){e}^{2x}}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{16}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{3{e}^{2x}}{4}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}[2(2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5)-2(3{x}^{2}+3x)+(6x+3)-3]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(4{x}^{3}+10)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(2{x}^{3}+5)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547947 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547977 1069 "q-126766" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547977 1071 "Given that $|C|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ use the general solution to the equation to find the function $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547977 1072 #uuid "11ed723f-8fe2-5c56-8bb0-841acdfb0940" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547977 1074 "The general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{2}+C) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(C) \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of ${y}^{′}=xy$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547848 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547978 1069 "q-73386" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547978 1071 "![](images/bd/bdce9d030776f147aa7a17881dc5369fc9e7f3a7f4776852d9f5d958ca526baa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $h(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$ given that:\n\n- $h(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to a$\n\n- $h(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to b$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547978 1072 #uuid "12aa5ec1-b59f-559a-80d0-644a88af964f" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547978 1074 "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-2$ the values of $h(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ \n\n![](images/02/02bb73c2c981a0fb9e128b89862b854f9fb6af6cca6ff216a69733561ae90dbf.png)\n\nSo, we can write: $h(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -2.$ \n\nTherefore, $a=-2.$\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4$ the values of $h(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ \n\n![](images/4c/4ce365f8ce995accf041aca6f1ac6ccc24d0b8e835e0d268c433ff244cfcfb10.png)\n\nSo, we can write: $h(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to 4.$ \n\nTherefore, $b=4.$\n\nFinally:\n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{4}{-2}=-2\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547884 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547979 1069 "q-51229" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547979 1071 "Evaluate $\\int ({x}^{3}+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547979 1072 #uuid "1627a960-4b89-5682-9e7a-8ebccade355e" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547979 1074 "For this integral we choose $u={x}^{3}+x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ Filling in the table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+x$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ |  |  |\n| $3{x}^{2}+1$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}$ | $+$ | $({x}^{3}+x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}$ |\n| $6x$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}$ | $-$ | $(3{x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}$ |\n| $6$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{8}$ | $+$ | $-\\frac{3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{16}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{3}+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{3}+x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+(3{x}^{2}+1)\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}+C \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}-x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+(6{x}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547879 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547980 1069 "q-39502" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547980 1071 "![](images/e3/e3dc597ee47ba7b86f51b37725a0998cb23b148a23448f8c6db21cbb60a0c350.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown above. What are the equations of its vertical asymptotes?" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547980 1072 #uuid "1705d8ca-0ba8-5feb-91d5-1e7a97ff9132" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547980 1074 "![](images/73/73bbd191451c2b7769ccd2903fd0e6c9da81a11853071bbfbed9711267903b9e.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$\n\nWe also see that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ And, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{1}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -{1}^{+}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {1}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {1}^{+}$\n\nThese give us the asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=1.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547873 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547981 1069 "q-39686" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547981 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547981 1072 #uuid "1938b4fe-a4da-5df4-945e-a4ba43cb2798" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547981 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3x-8} & =x+2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{-3x-8})}^{2} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ -3x-8 & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+(3x+12) & =0 \\\\ x(x+4)+3(x+4) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-4$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-4)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-4+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-4$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=-3$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-3)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-3+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 1 & \\ne -1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-3$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547878 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547982 1069 "q-126765" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547982 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation. Express your answer in the form $y=y(x).$\n\n$y\\,\\,=\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C),\\,\\,$where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547982 1072 #uuid "1bc80b6f-e02e-58f0-97b4-8ea651af23ac" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547982 1074 "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $y=y(x)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547908 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547917 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547983 1069 "q-340803" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547983 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547983 1072 #uuid "1c3a98af-770d-5596-b478-235b18fb3ea6" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547983 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547920 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547984 1069 "q-94941" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547984 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the plane $2z-x+1=0$ onto the $xy$-plane?" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547984 1072 #uuid "1c9e9df7-050b-54af-a7dc-34ce03f716ac" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547984 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2z-x+1=0\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x+3{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+3{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+3{y}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipse centered at $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547847 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547985 1069 "q-348879" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547985 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547985 1072 #uuid "1d4ef9d9-890a-5da6-8625-b613890df574" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547985 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{4}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{6x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{4}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6})(\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4I \\\\ 5I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{30}({ln}^{5}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}({ln}^{5}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{5}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5}-{0}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}({3}^{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547940 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547986 1069 "q-348848" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547986 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547986 1072 #uuid "1d5e523f-cb45-5cb6-96e4-37ea7521523c" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547986 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{4}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{4}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3})(\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4I \\\\ 5I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{15}({ln}^{5}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}({ln}^{5}⁡({e}^{5})-{ln}^{5}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}((5\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5}-{0}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}({5}^{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547851 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547949 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547950 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1069 "q-126770" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1071 "Calculate the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,⋯\\,.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1072 #uuid "1f6c3d65-e721-5449-9ddc-958a81a54048" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547987 1074 "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nWriting the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{10}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{9}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547923 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547988 1069 "q-12924" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547988 1071 "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547988 1072 #uuid "204edd9f-19f2-513e-ac33-9db65e721229" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547988 1074 "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547964 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547989 1069 "q-17605" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547989 1071 "![](images/a6/a66ac2663dadacc0648b5a8993df40edafe8c7c2ee9dddac4ef932203b99dbd7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547989 1072 #uuid "28d08449-f19d-5dae-9ff9-1b1d3bcb8500" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547989 1074 "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5de147e69639b8cfbbe591241603d437650b88d523058df09dd30b60a6482da.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547895 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547910 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547990 1069 "q-250220" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547990 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+x+4$ and the line $y=5x$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547990 1072 #uuid "28de8699-3497-5296-9483-734e67042dfa" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547990 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+4 \\\\ y=5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+4 & =5x \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=2.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x \\\\  & =5(2) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only point of intersection is $(2,10).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/268de319ae56302e9b633e09a4c74a0ebd62f3d8ed47829f709036082a644bf4.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547889 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547991 1069 "q-159373" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547991 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=5,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=10,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=16$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=31.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547991 1072 #uuid "29dbfdc5-a896-5333-926c-2c4e2687cdd6" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547991 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =16-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =31-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =10-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547882 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547992 1069 "q-340760" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547992 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic extensions of some functions from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of $-5f(x)-2g(x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547992 1072 #uuid "2c275290-69db-55d0-83e9-26518022c1c5" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547992 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)∼\\frac{\\alpha {a}_{0}+\\beta {c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\alpha {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-5f(x)-2g(x)$ are\n\n- $-5{a}_{0}-2{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-5{a}_{n}-2{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-5{b}_{n}-2{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of $-5f(x)-2g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-5{a}_{0}-2{c}_{0}.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547844 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547993 1069 "q-159392" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547993 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.2,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547993 1072 #uuid "2c722f59-6d05-5224-b152-d5620b69f9be" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547993 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =3.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =3.6-{1.2}^{2} \\\\  & =2.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(3)(0)f(3,0)+(1)(3)f(1,3)+(3)(3)f(3,3) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.3+9\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.8-2\\cdot 1.2 \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.6 & 2.16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547941 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547994 1069 "q-81958" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547994 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(10,8)$ lies on the curve $y=2f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547994 1072 #uuid "370ca68a-a4c7-585b-aa83-7cfb70ebc27e" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547994 1074 "The function $y=2f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe point $Q(10,q)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $P(10,8)$ on $y=2f(x).$ Since we know that the $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $2$ times the $y$-coordinate of $Q,$ we can form the following equation:\n\n$$\n2q=8\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get $q=4.$ Therefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(10,4).$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547869 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547995 1069 "q-19982" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547995 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{10}=23$ and the common difference $d=3$, what is the general formula for the $n$th term of the sequence?" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547995 1072 #uuid "3a1942e4-caa0-5f58-aeeb-9eef16c2b831" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547995 1074 "Since ${a}_{10}=23$ is $9$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{10} & ={a}_{1}+9d \\\\ 23 & ={a}_{1}+9(3) \\\\ 23 & ={a}_{1}+27 \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =-4+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547857 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547880 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547931 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1069 "q-340659" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1071 "Let $F(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{3}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1072 #uuid "3db32c22-3de8-5264-9b28-9916a3aeb5f2" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547996 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547936 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547997 1069 "q-111174" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547997 1071 "![](images/04/04a4c0b720676f48d1587c2e8ff0cd7096868ef0192738812af09bba725fd224.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547997 1072 #uuid "46986aeb-b3ed-5a20-9bf3-78659d9de421" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547997 1074 "To graph the function $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7f418df59b24e1410b172f362c087241b85c0fa9861231ec352f80cbc0668ff.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547942 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547998 1069 "q-159406" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547998 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.55$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2.2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.4,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547998 1072 #uuid "498cfd6b-9969-58ee-9468-55dacd0e9142" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547998 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.55$ | $0.25$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.8+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.2-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)f(2,1)+(3)(1)f(3,1)+(2)(2)f(2,2)+(3)(2)f(3,2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.55+3\\cdot 0.05+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =3.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.15-2.2\\cdot 1.4 \\\\  & =0.07.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.16 & 0.07 \\\\ 0.07 & 0.24\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547930 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547999 1069 "q-66881" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547999 1071 "Solve for $y$ where ${9}^{y}={27}^{y-3}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547999 1072 #uuid "49bb96bd-7826-5c22-b9da-330c6f2a3a6b" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186547999 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={3}^{2}$ and $27={3}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{2})}^{y}={({3}^{3})}^{y-3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2y}={3}^{3(y-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3(y-3) \\\\ 2y & =3y-9 \\\\ y & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547902 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548000 1069 "q-149608" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548000 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548000 1072 #uuid "4bc6d1d2-09ee-5cec-a2ab-850e837a407d" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548000 1074 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =2y(1-0) \\\\  & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547927 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548001 1069 "q-350641" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548001 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548001 1072 #uuid "4c0fb001-b215-5979-89f5-05a01ae52266" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548001 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}}{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{9})}{(1-\\frac{1}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{8}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547875 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548002 1069 "q-12736" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548002 1071 "![](images/38/38b95a03577313501d73df19170cdc82a50e65a6908f5fe99b1006f524134560.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following gives the plot of $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548002 1072 #uuid "4cedaa39-4caa-5cdd-aa21-8e5922b0f8c0" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548002 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $x$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/27/274973fd4068d7d509f735ad01775b25ae55102ae2c5d9aa6469b0043c598fe9.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547935 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548003 1069 "q-159419" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548003 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}48y(x-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548003 1072 #uuid "4f28747a-e241-5f89-b726-699a85c63f84" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548003 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48{y}^{3}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}(x{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{160}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{3}{320}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48x{y}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{5}{192}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}-(\\frac{4}{5})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547859 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548004 1069 "q-115002" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548004 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548004 1072 #uuid "509669b5-ef46-5eb1-8d54-7bbaca5f8fc9" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548004 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})))-(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-0}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547867 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548005 1069 "q-35921" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548005 1071 "![](images/fc/fc6f40458a9a38615b9d1fe01c7e454611a56915d2dcaadaa8661c4d56defb6f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548005 1072 #uuid "50fa8042-1b8b-5648-8556-3cccc6bf538e" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548005 1074 "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function is not defined. So we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547892 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548006 1069 "q-12889" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548006 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4)}^{4}=A{x}^{8}+B{x}^{6}+C{x}^{4}+128{x}^{2}+256\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, find the value of $A+B+C.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548006 1072 #uuid "510f41a7-5712-55db-b50d-5c80967bb9fb" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548006 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ and $b=4.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4)}^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{4}(4)^{0}+4{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{3}{(4)}^{1}+6{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{2}{(4)}^{2} \\\\  & \\,+4{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{1}{(4)}^{3}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{0}{(4)}^{4} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{16})(1)+4(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{8})(4)+6(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})(16) \\\\  & \\,+4(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(64)+1(1)(256) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8}}{16}+\\frac{16{x}^{6}}{8}+\\frac{96{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{256{x}^{2}}{2}+256 \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8}}{16}+2{x}^{6}+24{x}^{4}+128{x}^{2}+256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $A=\\frac{1}{16},B=2$ and $C=24.$ So, finally\n\n$$\nA+B+C=\\frac{1}{16}+2+24=\\frac{417}{16}.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547870 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548007 1069 "q-159402" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548007 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{4},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548007 1072 #uuid "53e9c56c-8467-523e-bd98-6ac7236cb57e" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548007 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{4}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-{(0)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{33}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}-{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [-1,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547894 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548008 1069 "q-362919" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548008 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}=(x-2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{3!}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548008 1072 #uuid "598873cf-7c1c-5ce4-9b98-2bbec96c77b0" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548008 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547961 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548009 1069 "q-50872" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548009 1071 "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-1)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-3,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series converges at $x=5$\n\nII. The series diverges at $x=-4$\n\nIII. The series converges at $x=-2$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548009 1072 #uuid "654aa15f-b5e0-5714-95bd-cdd476eb0257" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548009 1074 "The center of the expansion is $x=1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-3,5).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-3,5).$\n\n![](images/1f/1f6d98b7983946bf60df9bb29bc527af17a63a38834457404c5284b19165e7d5.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $5\\notin [-3,5),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-4\\notin [-3,5),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $-2\\in [-3,5),$ the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547953 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548010 1069 "q-23909" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548010 1071 "Consider the equation ${x}^{2}+1=k.$ For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548010 1072 #uuid "6679348c-4b94-5c71-ad1e-c5e2e39219c8" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548010 1074 "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-1} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\nk-1<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-1 & <0 \\\\ k & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $k$ that is less than $1$ will give an equation with no real solutions." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547960 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548011 1069 "q-159401" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548011 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=1.6$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=0.8,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548011 1072 #uuid "67d9ef63-b4c5-5251-99ba-45e5490b628a" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548011 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.8-{0.8}^{2} \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(2)(0)f(2,0)+(1)(1)f(1,1)+(2)(1)f(2,1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.3-1.6\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.24 & 0.02 \\\\ 0.02 & 0.16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547915 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548012 1069 "q-37453" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548012 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & y{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(0,0)<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(0,0)$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548012 1072 #uuid "698b79dc-de1f-5472-8b54-18da46bc32a4" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548012 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & y{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=y\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2})=(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2})=(1+{y}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2})=xy\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & xy\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} \\\\ xy\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & (1+{y}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={1}^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547954 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548013 1069 "q-26373" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548013 1071 "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${27}^{x+2}={3}^{4x}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548013 1072 #uuid "6adee969-6d90-563b-a4ef-2a55fc2a8432" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548013 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $27$ as ${3}^{3},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{27}^{x+2} & ={3}^{4x} \\\\ {({3}^{3})}^{x+2} & ={3}^{4x} \\\\ {3}^{3(x+2)} & ={3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+2) & =4x \\\\ 3x+6 & =4x \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547966 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548014 1069 "q-110676" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548014 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{(2s-{u}^{2})}^{4}=16{s}^{4}+A{s}^{3}{u}^{2}+B{s}^{2}{u}^{4}+Cs{u}^{6}+{u}^{8}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548014 1072 #uuid "700411f4-106e-5592-a547-8738d64ef693" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548014 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=2s,$ and $b=-{u}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2s-{u}^{2})^{4} & =1{a}^{4}{b}^{0}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(2s)^{4}{(-{u}^{2})}^{0}+4(2s)^{3}{(-{u}^{2})}^{1}+6(2s)^{2}{(-{u}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & \\,+4(2s)^{1}{(-{u}^{2})}^{3}+1(2s)^{0}{(-{u}^{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =1(16{s}^{4})(1)+4(8{s}^{3})(-{u}^{2})+6(4{s}^{2})({u}^{4}) \\\\  & \\,+4(2s)(-{u}^{6})+1(1)({u}^{8}) \\\\  & =16{s}^{4}-32{s}^{3}{u}^{2}+24{s}^{2}{u}^{4}-8s{u}^{6}+{u}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=-32,B=24,$ and $C=-8.$ Finally, then\n\n$$\nA+B+C=-32+24-8=-16.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547891 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548015 1069 "q-351689" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548015 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}=\\frac{x+3}{2}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{2}^{4}}+\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{{2}^{9}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548015 1072 #uuid "7026529b-9d4a-5618-98a8-c1bbd9c1f097" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548015 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}}{\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}{(x+3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x+3}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}-{n}^{2}}}| \\\\  & =|x+3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{2n+1}} \\\\  & =|x+3|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547871 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548016 1069 "q-159422" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548016 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{16}({x}^{2}-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{3},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{20}{21},$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=\\frac{40}{27},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548016 1072 #uuid "7039fb00-3d4a-54d6-a4b0-1bcf58d49ef2" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548016 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{4}-y{x}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[y{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{6}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{[\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{Var}[Y].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{27}-{(\\frac{20}{21})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{760}{1\\,323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}xy({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}x]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{7})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{8}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-(\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot (\\frac{20}{21}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547900 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548017 1069 "q-3402" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548017 1071 "Calculate $\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548017 1072 #uuid "706f56b6-76a3-5149-b5e7-35082287eff9" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548017 1074 "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-J \\\\ I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547904 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548018 1069 "q-159366" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548018 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=6,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=7,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=40,$ and variances $\\text{Var}[X]=2$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=5.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548018 1072 #uuid "72d6fd40-f077-555f-a2ea-48615443e754" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548018 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nUsing the above formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =40-6\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547938 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548019 1069 "q-124590" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548019 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548019 1072 #uuid "7ac43aed-fdf1-5439-85aa-b688b91d01e4" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548019 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{81}{625})=\\frac{3}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547845 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548020 1069 "q-79428" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548020 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-6,9)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548020 1072 #uuid "7ec57449-f520-50b3-b823-1f98b4f880f3" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548020 1074 "The function $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-6,9)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(3\\cdot (-6),9)=(-18,9),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547850 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548021 1069 "q-103827" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548021 1071 "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }$ for $-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548021 1072 #uuid "8023cebc-048b-5288-9c32-1da095db126f" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548021 1074 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1+{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\theta +{e}^{\\theta })^{2}+(1+{e}^{\\theta })^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta {e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }+1+2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2{e}^{\\theta }(\\theta +1)+2{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547955 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548022 1069 "q-67300" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548022 1071 "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548022 1072 #uuid "84522aa8-0251-51f3-8f96-c1848baee4b0" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548022 1074 "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547852 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548023 1069 "q-159299" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548023 1071 "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & b \\\\ c & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ is given above. Find $a+b+c.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548023 1072 #uuid "847972c0-6f90-54f3-90b0-10ccc1176f30" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548023 1074 "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=1$\n\n- $b={Σ}_{23}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{32}=1$\n\n- $c={Σ}_{31}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{13}=0$\n\nTherefore, $a+b+c=1+1+0=2.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547962 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548024 1069 "q-126769" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548024 1071 "Given that the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!},\n$$\n\ncalculate the radius of convergence $R$ of this power series.\n\n$R\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548024 1072 #uuid "8679792d-1ee6-50ba-bf59-17896dc979de" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548024 1074 "To find the radius of convergence, we apply the ratio test.\n\nSo, let's denote ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{4n+6}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{1}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547952 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548025 1069 "q-100839" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548025 1071 "Find the general formula for the $n$th term of the arithmetic sequence that begins with \n\n$$\n19,\\,17,\\,15,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548025 1072 #uuid "89937c4c-b18e-5039-ba6e-7940ef191d3c" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548025 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFrom the first few terms\n\n$$\n19,\\,17,\\,15,\\,…\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is $f(1)=19$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=17-19=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =19+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =19-2n+2 \\\\  & =21-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=21-2n.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547921 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548026 1069 "q-159304" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548026 1071 "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & a \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ is given above. Find $a.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548026 1072 #uuid "89c1f4fa-b7a3-52ff-a3e0-636f4aa1573d" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548026 1074 "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=1$\n\nTherefore, $a=1.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547909 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548027 1069 "q-152279" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548027 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x+2$ and the line $y=2-2x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548027 1072 #uuid "8dc89317-431f-536c-84f1-f80a52b02432" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548027 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x+2 \\\\ y=2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+2 & =2-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ x(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=0.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2-2{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2-2(-4) \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,10).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2-2{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2-2(0) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=10,\\,{y}_{2}=2,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=10+2=12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/94/941d698059cfd145f263cbdde048fc4a32245cdad4bc6bb6d7a504d005b6c231.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547912 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548028 1069 "q-14755" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548028 1071 "Evaluate $\\int (2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548028 1072 #uuid "8eec90d6-f38c-50f0-b171-7f37a87f27a1" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548028 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$ Filling in the table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ |  |  |\n| $6{x}^{2}+6x$ | $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ | $+$ | $-(2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ |\n| $12x+6$ | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ | $-$ | $(6{x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ |\n| $12$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ | $+$ | $(12x+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ |\n| $0$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ | $-$ | $-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-(2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+(6{x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(12x+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-12x-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(6{x}^{2}+6x-12)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547855 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548029 1069 "q-39498" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548029 1071 "![](images/ed/ed3ed1aeb01e59fae6efa312fbbdfb476fa493df5a1dd5730e4e5c595827a8ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is true when $x\\to -{2}^{-}?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548029 1072 #uuid "936ec21a-6fd7-54a1-80fb-a5b7564ca5ca" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548029 1074 "The notation $x\\to -{2}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $-2$ *from the left*.\n\n![](images/ef/ef61a0d8a90ec9d0b120b0b9bf47077c7c77887f457182c09ee36b7d78b8cf9f.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{-}$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547906 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548030 1069 "q-152314" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548030 1071 "Given that the line $y=x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548030 1072 #uuid "95764a8d-23d1-5507-836a-2817991f65d9" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548030 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y=x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & =x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+1-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=-1,$ and $c=1-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-1)\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{1-4+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{4k-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k-3 & <0 \\\\ 4k & <3 \\\\ k & <\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547926 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548031 1069 "q-108425" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548031 1071 "Find the $33$rd term of the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n13,\\,8,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548031 1072 #uuid "985912e6-de0a-5f5e-a520-8f2ffaee1968" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548031 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n13,\\,8,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,…\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=13$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=8-13=-5.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =13+(n-1)(-5) \\\\  & =13-5n+5 \\\\  & =18-5n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $33$rd term. All we have to do is substitute $n=33:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =18-5n \\\\ {a}_{33} & =18-5(33) \\\\  & =18-165 \\\\  & =-147\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $33$rd term of the sequence is ${a}_{33}=-147.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547901 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548032 1069 "q-104069" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548032 1071 "$\\int 2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548032 1072 #uuid "98841fb8-b49b-5f0b-816e-9b78835d046c" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548032 1074 "For this integral we choose $u=2{x}^{3}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Filling in the table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{3}$ | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ |  |  |\n| $6{x}^{2}$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}$ | $+$ | $-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ |\n| $12x$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}$ |\n| $12$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{16}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C \\\\  & =(3x-2{x}^{3})\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+(6{x}^{2}-3)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548033 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547862 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548033 1069 "q-159407" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548033 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{8}{5},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548033 1072 #uuid "9895539f-08a9-52d3-aba6-0550c7a4d108" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548033 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[0,1]\\times [0,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=2x$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547948 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548034 1069 "q-126764" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548034 1071 "Use Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(0.2)$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n$y\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548034 1072 #uuid "9b186f94-2456-5e8b-8019-128c4ccb5b71" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548034 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(0.2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=0.2$ is $0.2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0.2-0}{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 0.1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+0.1=0.1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0.1\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0.2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0.2\\cdot 0.1=0.02 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0.1+0.1=0.2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0.02=0.02\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.02$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.02$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $f(0.2)≈0.02.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547965 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548035 1069 "q-13148" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548035 1071 "$\\int ({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548035 1072 #uuid "9ecd3f37-138d-5c59-a588-9da4d5939fe0" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548035 1074 "For this integral we choose $u={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ Filling in the table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ |  |  |\n| $3{x}^{2}+6x$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}$ |\n| $6x+6$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}$ | $-$ | $\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}$ |\n| $6$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{8}$ | $+$ | $-\\frac{(3x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{16}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{(3x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}+C \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x-3)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+(6{x}^{2}+12x-3)\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547899 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548036 1069 "q-83252" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548036 1071 "Find the average acceleration of a particle on the interval $[0,2]$ if its acceleration in ${\\text{mm/s}}^{2}$ is given by the function $a(t)={e}^{t}+2t,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548036 1072 #uuid "a23536ce-7cba-5974-838f-a84131e7f6d9" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548036 1074 "To calculate the average value of the acceleration on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}\\,{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{t}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{t}+{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[({e}^{2}+{2}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{0}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}+4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average acceleration is $\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}+3)\\,{\\text{mm/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547914 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548037 1069 "q-126768" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548037 1071 "Using the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x),$ approximate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Write your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\,≈\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548037 1072 #uuid "a3b460f4-8f03-59ea-8d4e-3d88c9d8d99f" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548037 1074 "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ by integrating the above series term-by-term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ≈{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{42}{x}^{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{42}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547896 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548038 1069 "q-126763" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548038 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{″}(0).$\n\n${f}^{″}(0)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548038 1072 #uuid "a4bf276d-b40b-5d47-b840-e4ace9d966fe" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548038 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot 2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4{x}^{2}y+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the point $(0,0)$ lies on $f,$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & =-4\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+2\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547858 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548039 1069 "q-353947" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548039 1071 "A test cart moves in a straight line with velocity \n$v(t)=\\frac{3}{t},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds and $v$ is measured in feet per second.\n\nFind the average acceleration ${a}_{\\text{avg}}$ over the time interval $t\\in [1,3].$ Do not round your answer.\n\n${a}_{\\text{avg}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548039 1072 #uuid "a7420375-7675-57fc-8640-9f218c433ea5" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548039 1074 "The average acceleration is the average rate of change of the velocity, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{v(3)-v(1)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{3}{3}]-[\\frac{3}{1}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average acceleration is $-1\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547887 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548040 1069 "q-10951" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548040 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}=\\frac{1}{2}+(x+\\frac{1}{4})+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{4}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{5}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548040 1072 #uuid "a84f0335-631a-5413-a947-37ccb6b7bf94" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548040 1074 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.$Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n+1}}{n+3}\\cdot \\frac{n+2}{{3}^{n}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3}(x+\\frac{1}{4})| \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3} \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+\\frac{1}{4}|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547903 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548041 1069 "q-35267" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548041 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the plane $2x-y=0$ onto the $xz$-plane?" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548041 1072 #uuid "aad779ca-b824-50cb-b4dc-f0dd2ba06356" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548041 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2x-y=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(2x-1)^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}-4x+1 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}-5{x}^{2}+4x & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2}-5({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{4}{25})+\\frac{4}{5} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2}-5{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5{z}^{2}-25{(x-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbola centered at $(\\frac{2}{5},0)$ in the $xz$-plane." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547932 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548042 1069 "q-159394" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548042 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=1.7$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=0.6,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548042 1072 #uuid "b0df8fdd-91c9-54e0-8d8a-27e731147330" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548042 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =3.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =3.1-{1.7}^{2} \\\\  & =0.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.6-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(2)(0)f(2,0)+(1)(1)f(1,1)+(2)(1)f(2,1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1-1.7\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =-0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.21 & -0.02 \\\\ -0.02 & 0.24\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547846 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548043 1069 "q-39451" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548043 1071 "![](images/cd/cd6662d44cd356ee112fd0bec330d0cb85197935a312069ab766c46e0ef9c61d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. As $x\\to -{2}^{-},\\,$ $f(x)\\to \\infty $\n\nII. As $x\\to {3}^{-},\\,$ $f(x)\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. As $x\\to {3}^{+},\\,$ $f(x)\\to -\\infty $" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548043 1072 #uuid "b21470c3-1ee9-52ab-ab28-1768b780f6d2" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548043 1074 "The notation $x\\to -{2}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $-2$ *from the left*. In the same way, the notation $x\\to {3}^{-}$ means that $x$ approaches $3$ *from the left* while $x\\to {3}^{+}$ means that $x$ approaches $3$ *from the right*. \n\n![](images/37/37af0eeb28d271efa4bd8f296b8c530b82a7f368f82fa96e025695317b81519d.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that:\n\n- As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ do not approach $\\infty .$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- As $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ So, statement II is true.\n\n- As $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ do not approach $-\\infty .$ So, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547943 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548044 1069 "q-132849" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548044 1071 "In a factory, the average amount of sugar in a cookie is $15$ grams. However, $10%$ of cookies contain no more than $14$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the amount of sugar in a cookie, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548044 1072 #uuid "b642f61b-90d3-52e8-a698-ed0e6fdd52ac" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548044 1074 "Let $X$ represent the amount (in grams) of sugar in a cookie. Then we have $X∼N(15,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 14)=0.10.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 14) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{14-15}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{1}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)=-1.2816.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =-1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{1.2816} \\\\  & ≈0.78.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547928 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548045 1069 "q-111108" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548045 1071 "![](images/23/23015d804d6348d7ab6477fa981a1f2b24f43db4be96cca511ba53cfb53e49bd.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown above. What are the equations of its vertical asymptotes?" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548045 1072 #uuid "b6d62580-c7ef-590e-96ab-faddfa2655e8" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548045 1074 "Recall that $x=a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y=f(x)$ if $f(x)\\to \\infty $ or $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {a}^{+}$ or $x\\to {a}^{-}.$\n\n![](images/e6/e6d80874f9150371204f12b2e933085f9b2666ac77fce5430f9645e5af073a36.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nWe also see that as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ And, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{+}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}$\n\nThese give us the asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=0.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547944 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548046 1069 "q-146284" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548046 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|4}=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548046 1072 #uuid "b82a91db-40b3-51bf-b539-d94cf7913732" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548046 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)]-{\\mu }_{X|4}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547861 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548047 1069 "q-350640" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548047 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548047 1072 #uuid "b85d1cfe-34fb-5d55-ad84-699fc2c94f6d" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548047 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(1)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(2)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}={(-0.4)}^{2}=0.16.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}{1-0.16} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}(0.16)}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547860 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548048 1069 "q-214180" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548048 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $-2$ and the second term is $3.$\n\n$f(n)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,n\\ge 1$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548048 1072 #uuid "bc128731-698c-584b-a0e3-391ca3d39ded" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548048 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term $f(1)=-2.$ Also, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=3-(-2)=5.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-2+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =-2+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547934 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548049 1069 "q-13147" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548049 1071 "Evaluate $\\int (2{x}^{4}+4x){e}^{3x}\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548049 1072 #uuid "c451ec1c-6a2b-54c4-80d7-187b41f5f298" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548049 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u=2{x}^{4}+4x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{3x}.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{4}+4x$ | ${e}^{3x}$ |  |  |\n| $8{x}^{3}+4$ | $\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4x){e}^{3x}}{3}$ |\n| $24{x}^{2}$ | $\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+4){e}^{3x}}{9}$ |\n| $48x$ | $\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{27}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{8{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{9}$ |\n| $48$ | $\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{81}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{16x{e}^{3x}}{27}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{243}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{16{e}^{3x}}{81}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{4}+4x){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{81}(27(2{x}^{4}+4x)-9(8{x}^{3}+4)+9(8{x}^{2})-3(16x)+16)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{81}(54{x}^{4}-72{x}^{3}+72{x}^{2}+60x-20)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{81}(27{x}^{4}-36{x}^{3}+36{x}^{2}+30x-10)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547929 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548050 1069 "q-340768" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548050 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic extensions of some functions from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $6\\text{th}$ Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)-2g(x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548050 1072 #uuid "c68b418a-a9c2-512b-93fe-1e0b537572e9" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548050 1074 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)∼\\frac{\\alpha {a}_{0}+\\beta {c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\alpha {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)-2g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3{a}_{0}-2{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-3{a}_{n}-2{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-3{b}_{n}-2{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $6$th sine coefficient of $-3f(x)-2g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3{b}_{6}-2{d}_{6}.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547924 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548051 1069 "q-12891" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548051 1071 "Find, in descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(1-{z}^{2})}^{4}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548051 1072 #uuid "c9b934c2-8c57-56f1-8a65-42f138da7b11" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548051 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=1,$ and $b=-{z}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-{z}^{2})}^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(1)^{4}(-{z}^{2})^{0}+4(1)^{3}(-{z}^{2})^{1}+6(1)^{2}(-{z}^{2})^{2}+4(1)^{1}(-{z}^{2})^{3}+1(1)^{0}(-{z}^{2})^{4} \\\\  & =1(1)(1)+4(1)(-{z}^{2})+6(1)({z}^{4})+4(1)(-{z}^{6})+1(1)({z}^{8}) \\\\  & =1-4{z}^{2}+6{z}^{4}-4{z}^{6}+{z}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the binomial expansion in descending powers of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(1-{z}^{2})^{4}={z}^{8}-4{z}^{6}+6{z}^{4}-4{z}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion are\n\n$$\n{z}^{8}-4{z}^{6}+6{z}^{4}.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547946 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548052 1069 "q-110669" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548052 1071 "![](images/ed/ed4ddeb8ea4f2bb9d05dc05d3ff73559806186d66a78431e89f50e3bbe390dd5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is true when $x\\to 1?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548052 1072 #uuid "ca47eb18-4aa6-5c60-add3-13a91c37460f" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548052 1074 "![](images/2a/2a84c7f64371af4728f86d83df8680ab85683cf4cf564ca9622d93b39fdfc267.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$ the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547864 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548053 1069 "q-159369" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548053 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=1,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=4,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=10$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=4.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548053 1072 #uuid "cae81e4a-bed5-56e8-841e-be25ff76e753" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548053 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =10-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =4-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547916 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548054 1069 "q-159300" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548054 1071 "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ a & 2 & b \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ is given above. Find $a+b.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548054 1072 #uuid "cc3c12f1-dbf7-51f0-875a-0336c3cabe74" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548054 1074 "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{21}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{12}=1$\n\n- $b={Σ}_{23}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{32}=0$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=1+0=1.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547967 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548055 1069 "q-115005" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548055 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548055 1072 #uuid "ce3e4235-61fa-5c48-8fde-1e9523a76255" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548055 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-I \\\\ 2I & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ))-(\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }-{e}^{\\pi }}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547911 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548056 1069 "q-222414" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548056 1071 "Which value of $h$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}-h=2?$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548056 1072 #uuid "d0ec07f9-1330-5f73-b5fc-18b90684baa7" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548056 1074 "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-h & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2+h \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2+h} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{2+h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $h$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+h & =0 \\\\ h & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h=-2$ will give only one solution to the equation." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547890 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548057 1069 "q-340511" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548057 1071 "Let $F(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,6)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+12)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548057 1072 #uuid "d11bc330-9efc-55d1-a388-de4f864cc23e" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548057 1074 "Since $f$ is the $6$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,6).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+12)=f((x+6)+6)=f(x+6)=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(7)=f(1+6)=f(1)=F(1)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7)=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=7.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+3)=f(5)=F(5)=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+3)\\ne f(x)$ for $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547886 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547959 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547963 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1069 "q-126767" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,⋯\\,.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1072 #uuid "d1d89a0f-00b0-5608-ac35-e4c5c811a70d" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548058 1074 "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with ${x}^{2}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}) & =({x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{6}({x}^{2})^{3}+\\frac{1}{120}({x}^{2})^{5}+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{10}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547876 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548059 1069 "q-114951" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548059 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548059 1072 #uuid "d34958ec-5dd9-5a10-8bd6-7504a0d293c1" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548059 1074 "First, we let\n\n$$\nI=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{8}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8{ln}^{7}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{8}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{8{ln}^{7}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8I \\\\ 9I & ={ln}^{9}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547881 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548060 1069 "q-14773" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548060 1071 "$\\int 2{x}^{4}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548060 1072 #uuid "d35b604c-10fb-5e20-9163-ae0fba981c99" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548060 1074 "For this integral, we choose $u=2{x}^{4}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}.$ Filling in our table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{4}$ | ${e}^{4x}$ |  |  |\n| $8{x}^{3}$ | $\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{{x}^{4}{e}^{4x}}{2}$ |\n| $24{x}^{2}$ | $\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}}{2}$ |\n| $48x$ | $\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{64}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{3{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}{8}$ |\n| $48$ | $\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{256}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{3x{e}^{4x}}{16}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{1024}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{3{e}^{4x}}{64}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\int 2{x}^{4}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{64}(32{x}^{4}-32{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-12x+3)+C\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547874 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548061 1069 "q-39351" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548061 1071 "![](images/d9/d953b897affdad991b86db265b90610cb6e28f3ac6a94889c5143f2289d93f68.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Find the values ​​of $a$ such that $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to a.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548061 1072 #uuid "d362e2bc-26cf-51c4-91b6-e3e5ba9e9850" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548061 1074 "![](images/e9/e9862ea9f1debb162a62a3eb3422f2313f4a8512c30fe2a16e982207639c0ef4.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-3$ or $2$, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ \n\nSo, we can write: $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -3$ and $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$ \n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $a=2.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547907 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548062 1069 "q-79427" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548062 1071 "![](images/75/75f479ff700f106f5cae6bafa94df87381d5fc0fca1758aad55f6bc5be1d7c38.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548062 1072 #uuid "d363b88c-fb75-5b26-bd10-c56875f40c1e" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548062 1074 "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0f3cf395fe5f442212e057f73ef48ab458370e914424e186611c70f1c9f29953.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547898 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547925 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547937 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1069 "q-340658" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1071 "Let $F(x)=2{x}^{3}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{2}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1072 #uuid "dc02bcc8-5f0f-5f17-99b3-d1e75d3135c1" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548063 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547888 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548064 1069 "q-159302" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548064 1071 "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ a & b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ is given above. Find $a+b.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548064 1072 #uuid "dcf740fc-84e9-5215-a4db-974afa421f20" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548064 1074 "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{31}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{13}=0$\n\n- $b={Σ}_{32}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{23}=2$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=0+2=2.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547868 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548065 1069 "q-52265" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548065 1071 "Given that  $64-64w+16{w}^{2}=0$, find $w.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548065 1072 #uuid "e09c7bc1-cc56-5292-bdc6-0155dadb5e01" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548065 1074 "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64-64w+16{w}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {8}^{2}-2\\cdot 8\\cdot 4w+(4w)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (8-4w)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(8-4w)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |8-4w| & =0 \\\\ 8-4w & =0 \\\\ 4w & =8 \\\\ w & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547866 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548066 1069 "q-338943" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548066 1071 "![](images/e3/e30b6d047b119e5beb5f0d5cf5e013628e7c9836e0ca264ee2da92d1e589a0fc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548066 1072 #uuid "e2d9d433-ba35-5944-b803-b7c1dcd52280" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548066 1074 "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547913 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548067 1069 "q-14779" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548067 1071 "Evaluate  $\\int (2{x}^{4}+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548067 1072 #uuid "e3b2de25-1eea-5c88-bd03-1de1fe67ccfb" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548067 1074 "For this integral we choose $u=2{x}^{4}+4$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ Filling in the table gives the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same-color Products** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{4}+4$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ |  |  |\n| $8{x}^{3}$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}$ |\n| $24{x}^{2}$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}$ | $-$ | $\\frac{8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}$ |\n| $48x$ | $-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{27}$ | $+$ | $-\\frac{8{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}$ |\n| $48$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{81}$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{16x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{27}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{243}$ | $+$ | $\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{81}$ |\n\nWe add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{4}+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{16x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{27}+\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{81}+C \\\\  & =(54{x}^{4}-72{x}^{2}+124)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{81}+(24{x}^{3}-16x)\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{27}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547951 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548068 1069 "q-39754" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548068 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548068 1072 #uuid "e49f42fb-4ed5-5c34-8e9e-f41f753d3cfd" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548068 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x-9} & =x-1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{5x-9})}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ 5x-9 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+10 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & =0 \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=2$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(2)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}2-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=2$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=5$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(5)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}5-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=5$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1$  are $x=2,\\,5.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547958 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548069 1069 "q-12740" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548069 1071 "![](images/e9/e97cd82250cc2bfe7ab95e4bc0ec5b27aa6ea07267f4c668bfb2388eb9e6789b.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following gives the plot of $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548069 1072 #uuid "e5b892f7-97b8-58eb-8439-15e42863b653" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548069 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)$ from $y=f(x),$ we need to reflect the graph over the $x$ axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/c8/c862b42816402f12e9bd9cbd1c274ed4f3316bea57f8d8c9c3bcac28811a4e9b.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547849 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548070 1069 "q-146207" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548070 1071 "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ is shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|1}=3.3.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548070 1072 #uuid "e707f526-b15a-5f98-b415-f7cf137b340a" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548070 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[(2)^{2}\\cdot 0.35+(4)^{2}\\cdot 0.65]-{(3.3)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.4+10.4-10.89 \\\\  & =0.91.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547933 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548071 1069 "q-339105" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548071 1071 "![](images/0c/0cdb3e9c74a36e5ef13436b7b18968064b278f6d8e0dffc27820f618f7f4fee9.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548071 1072 #uuid "e817a4fb-b6c9-5bc7-b2be-45b00395342a" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548071 1074 "As $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty $, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547893 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548072 1069 "q-159371" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548072 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=3,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=6,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=9$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=17.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548072 1072 #uuid "ea8dc2eb-77b6-5a6b-972c-806dd20059a5" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548072 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =9-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =17-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =6-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547872 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548073 1069 "q-12742" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548073 1071 "![](images/6f/6f8de021f84bdbf874c48462bfa98521b77d1e040c740dce47cd54a323c07d98.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following gives the plot of $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548073 1072 #uuid "ec1f24da-3bcd-535c-a161-f60abdae11d2" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548073 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $x$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1ef9708b889ddfd153eb85d2f32c7a4511dbf112752f2d33afe009948e7e56e.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547897 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548074 1069 "q-159309" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548074 1071 "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & a \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ is given above. Find $a.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548074 1072 #uuid "ef075455-3a78-5d07-aadc-6948b6898570" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548074 1074 "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547877 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548075 1069 "q-353941" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548075 1071 "A robotic sled moves along a straight track with velocity \n$v(t)={t}^{3}-t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds and $v$ is measured in meters per second.\n\nFind the average acceleration ${a}_{\\text{avg}}$ over the time interval $t\\in [1,3].$ Do not round your answer.\n\n${a}_{\\text{avg}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548075 1072 #uuid "f05a5b67-91d9-5027-a739-e465c398f589" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548075 1074 "The average acceleration is the average rate of change of the velocity, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{v(b)-v(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(3)-v(1)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[(3)^{3}-(3)]-[(1)^{3}-(1)]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(27-3)-(1-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2} \\\\  & =12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average acceleration is $12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547905 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548076 1069 "q-83246" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548076 1071 "A golden eagle is moving in a straight line with a velocity of $v(t)=\\frac{{2}^{t}}{12}\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the average velocity of the golden eagle on the interval $[2,6].$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548076 1072 #uuid "f371caa1-75a3-5280-8f9d-dfa2d56f63d8" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548076 1074 "To calculate the average value of the velocity on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{6-2}\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{2}^{t}}{12})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{t}){|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{6}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{48\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average velocity is $\\frac{5}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547865 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548077 1069 "q-159396" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548077 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=5$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=2.7,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548077 1072 #uuid "f655f9f5-f0a0-522e-aa7b-2faf26b5e6dc" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548077 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.65$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =28-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.65+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =8.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =8.2-{2.7}^{2} \\\\  & =0.91.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(2)f(2,2)+(6)(2)f(6,2)+(2)(4)f(2,4)+(6)(4)f(6,4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.15+12\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.1+24\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =13.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =13.4-5\\cdot 2.7 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0.91\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547939 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548078 1069 "q-340781" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548078 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $7$th sine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548078 1072 #uuid "f98d94e0-3737-5c43-aa65-d5af57241d7a" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548078 1074 "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n}$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $7$th sine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is $-{b}_{7}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547957 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548079 1069 "q-353482" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548079 1071 "The subscriber count $P(t)$ of a streaming service follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2P(5\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=300.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the subscriber count growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548079 1072 #uuid "fbdb7241-60ea-5f22-afcd-5e46de779c9a" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548079 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=5000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $5000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5000=2500.\n$$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547856 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548080 1069 "q-50028" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548080 1071 "A fly’s position relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by the function $x(t)=\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}t)\\,\\text{m},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes. Find its average position of the fly between $0$ and $2$ minutes." 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548080 1072 #uuid "fbeb0099-1c78-5b43-8e43-b8ec47f11368" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548080 1074 "To calculate the average value of the position on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}\\,{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}t))\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}t))|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\cdot 2))-(\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\cdot 0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average position is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547853 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547956 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548081 1069 "q-250103" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548081 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+1$ and the line $y=3x$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548081 1072 #uuid "fc9c836c-676e-5aa5-b4fe-1701bb866c98" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548081 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x+1 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+1 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}+5x+1-3x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-1.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x \\\\  & =3(-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only point of intersection is $(-1,-3).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a5db47f31fc3e7be1afa4c5413e193c0751b68998e573093c69da5f2441b5a7.png)" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548082 1058 #edb/ref 17592186548112 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548082 1062 #uuid "00bf7e71-a2f6-5d03-9ce5-51e605b39bdf" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548082 1063 #edb/ref 17592186510376 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548